WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.009 --> 00:00:17.070
THE STATE OF VOX:
At first glance, Vox is an extraordinarily

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:19.939
successful media organization.

00:00:19.939 --> 00:00:25.160
On YouTube alone, they have 11 million subscribers
and over 3 billion views.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:29.560
And in the last 30 days itself, they’ve
gained over 20 million views and a hundred

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:31.119
thousand subscribers.

00:00:31.119 --> 00:00:35.800
Given the high quality journalism and documentaries
that come out of Vox, it’s not surprising

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:37.550
that they’re so successful.

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:43.230
But, what may be surprising is that there’s
a large community that can’t stand Vox.

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:48.350
They believe that Vox skews perspectives and
that much of their content centers around

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:51.690
garnering attention as opposed to truly informing.

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:56.300
It’s not just some viewers that are put
off by their content either, many journalists

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:57.750
are as well.

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:01.850
Just one Google seach will bring up dozens
of opinion pieces trashing on Vox.

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:06.330
But, much of this, you can just write off
as natural criticism.

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:11.240
Whenever an organization gets relatively large,
its gonna attract detracters regardless of

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:12.430
what they do.

00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:17.410
But, something that we can’t write off as
simply is the exodus at Vox.

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:23.470
Johnny Harris, is likely the most notable
example, but he’s by no means the only one.

00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:26.830
Another Vox journalist turned YouTuber is
Cleo Abram.

00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:30.240
It’s not just journalists who are leaving
Vox either.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:33.380
This exodus actually goes all the way to the
top.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:39.030
In just the last few years, 2 out of the 3
Vox founders left the company.

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:43.979
And it’s not like these guys completely
quit journalism or the media industry either.

00:01:43.979 --> 00:01:45.930
They just quit Vox.

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:50.030
Matthew Yglesias, for example, left so that
he could become a writer at a paid newsletter

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:51.159
called Substack.

00:01:51.159 --> 00:01:56.160
Similarly, Ezra Klein left Vox to become a
notable journalist at the New York Times.

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:59.170
Yet another executive who ditched Vox is Laruen
Williams.

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:04.950
While she wasn’t a cofounder, she did serve
as editor in chief and senior vice president.

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:10.379
Too make things worse, all three of these
departures happened in the same month, November

00:02:10.379 --> 00:02:11.420
of 2020.

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:16.030
There’s also a good chance that Vox is dealing
with some financial challenges throughout

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:19.690
this recession given that they laid of 39
people last year.

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:24.020
So, what happened to Vox?

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:31.930
DARING BEGINNINGS:
Taking a look back, Ezra Klein wasn’t always

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:33.390
looking to ditch Vox.

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:37.900
In fact, not too long ago, he was looking
to ditch the Washington Post.

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:42.560
Ezra was the head of a public policy blog
where he regularly analyzed and discussed

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:45.110
upcoming legislation and the political landscape.

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:49.650
While this was a pretty safe position to be
in, Ezra felt the need to address a shortfall

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:50.910
within the market.

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:59.120
He felt that the traditional media outlets
weren’t providing in ea to upper management.

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:04.069
But, after they rejected his idea, it didn’t
take him long to leave, and he took several

00:03:04.069 --> 00:03:08.470
Washington Post employees with him including
one of Vox’s other co founders: Melissa

00:03:08.470 --> 00:03:09.470
Bell.

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:14.600
Together, they hired a team of 20 people each
of whom were an expert in a given topic area,

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:20.950
and shortly after, they launched Vox on April
6, 2014 with the goal of making the news exciting

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:21.950
again.

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:25.590
Their initial content focused on explaining
various facets of the Affordable Care Act

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:26.620
or Obamacare.

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:31.790
They also spent time exploring economic issues
like the massive public debt.

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:36.840
These articles and videos performed quite
well especially for a brand new media organization.

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:42.150
But, it would take Vox a few months to really
trigger the algorithm which brings us to their

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:45.610
first viral hit: Three big reasons war is
going away.

00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:50.670
They followed this up with yet another viral
hit just a few weeks later titled “Weed

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:52.670
is not more dangerous than alcohol”.

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:58.170
Pretty quickly, Vox started to build a name
for themselves as the edgy media outlet that

00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:00.159
wasn’t afraid to make their positions clear.

00:04:00.159 --> 00:04:04.710
I mean, they were literally claiming that
the US debt wasn’t a big deal and that weed

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:06.970
shouldn’t be ostracized.

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:11.590
They even had videos comparing Bitcoin to
the internet and debunking Shark Week.

00:04:11.590 --> 00:04:16.690
While many of these were great one off videos,
Vox was yet to find a core series that people

00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:18.220
would tune in for.

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:24.270
But, this would change on July 23, 2014 when
Vox posted a video called “What made North

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:26.190
Korea so bizarre”.

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:31.120
Vice had already popularized the elusive nature
of North Korea across YouTube.

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:37.830
So when Vox posted a video about North Korea,
it instantly went viral, and since then, North

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:42.270
Korea based content has become a staple on
the channel garnering tens of millions of

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:43.389
views.

00:04:43.389 --> 00:04:47.631
It looks like this is one of the things that
Johnny Harris took with him from Vox as he

00:04:47.631 --> 00:04:50.410
has his own multi million view North Korea
video.

00:04:50.410 --> 00:04:55.291
Speaking of Johnny Harris, he was an early
employee at Vox having joined in 2014 as a

00:04:55.291 --> 00:04:56.770
senior video producer.

00:04:56.770 --> 00:05:01.259
Aside from producing videos about North Korea,
Johnny worked on producing map based videos,

00:05:01.259 --> 00:05:02.509
something that he’s since become known for.

00:05:02.509 --> 00:05:06.259
Eventually, Johnny would merge the elusive
aspect of North Korea with the geographical

00:05:06.259 --> 00:05:08.130
aspect of maps.

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:12.310
And the combination of these two sides would
lead to the creation of one of Vox’ most

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:18.699
popular docu series of all time: Borders which
brings us into the glory days of Vox.

00:05:18.699 --> 00:05:25.539
THE GLORY DAYS:
In the late 2010s, Vox was killing it.

00:05:25.539 --> 00:05:29.949
If you go to their channel and sort by most
popular, you’ll see that virtually all of

00:05:29.949 --> 00:05:33.310
their most popular videos were posted during
this time period.

00:05:33.310 --> 00:05:37.889
Not only did they have deep thought provoking
content about various hot topics, but they

00:05:37.889 --> 00:05:40.730
delivered said content in an exceptional way.

00:05:40.730 --> 00:05:44.300
This is something that hasn’t changed at
Vox to this day.

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:50.169
The quality of their animations, infographics,
and editing is top notch for YouTube, and

00:05:50.169 --> 00:05:53.800
honestly its better than even most traditional
media outlets.

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:57.590
You could say that Vox had achieved their
goal of making informative videos not just

00:05:57.590 --> 00:06:01.710
watchable or informative but full on entertaining.

00:06:01.710 --> 00:06:07.200
With every video, there was a beginning, middle,
and end with main characters, failures, and

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:08.460
successes.

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:14.020
Each video was a story, a journey through
a specific topic or historical event.

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:18.560
Something that made Vox even more popular
was the downfall of the cable TV.

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:22.870
More and more people were switching completely
streaming platforms like Netflix and Hulu.

00:06:22.870 --> 00:06:28.021
But, while these platforms had super exciting
shows and long form documentaries, something

00:06:28.021 --> 00:06:30.610
that they lacked was news based content.

00:06:30.610 --> 00:06:35.919
And all of this combined led to the extraordinary
rise of what we all know as Vox today.

00:06:35.919 --> 00:06:38.020
But, wait a minute.

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:39.610
Where’s the bad stuff?

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:44.050
If Vox was producing such great content, why
do a large portion of viewers and journalists

00:06:44.050 --> 00:06:45.330
hate Vox?

00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:48.970
And why are leading hosts and even founders
leaving the company?

00:06:48.970 --> 00:06:54.460
Well, so far, I’ve only been telling one
side of Vox’s story.

00:06:54.460 --> 00:06:59.889
Throughout this entire journey, Vox had another
side that was not nearly as respected.

00:06:59.889 --> 00:07:05.009
And I think the best way to explain this other
side is as the BuzzFeed side.

00:07:05.009 --> 00:07:08.729
If you’re not familiar with BuzzFeed, they’re
another popular media company that gained

00:07:08.729 --> 00:07:11.870
much of their fame during the same time period
as Vox.

00:07:11.870 --> 00:07:16.050
Instead of focusing on news based content
though, BuzzFeed focused on entertainment

00:07:16.050 --> 00:07:20.479
and experiences, and they were infamous for
their clickbait BS articles.

00:07:20.479 --> 00:07:23.139
Here’s a few just to give you an idea.

00:07:23.139 --> 00:07:27.410
I Cannot Believe The Haircut Grimes Just Gave
Her And Elon Musk's Baby.

00:07:27.410 --> 00:07:30.120
10 Signs That Your Cat Is A Wizard.

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:32.800
Which Ousted Arab Spring Ruler Are You?

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:37.099
Given that Vox was in a much more serious
informative niche, you would think that they

00:07:37.099 --> 00:07:40.419
would be above such articles, but they weren’t.

00:07:40.419 --> 00:07:41.710
Who is Solange?

00:07:41.710 --> 00:07:43.949
And why is she attacking Jay Z?

00:07:43.949 --> 00:07:46.069
Jay-Z called lacrosse "soft."

00:07:46.069 --> 00:07:48.120
The research literature disagrees.

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:50.949
The time when hip-hop's top drag queen met
RuPaul.

00:07:50.949 --> 00:07:55.690
In many of these cases, the writers knew that
the articles were ridiculous.

00:07:55.690 --> 00:08:00.740
But, they posted them anyway because they
also knew that they could leverage these celebrities

00:08:00.740 --> 00:08:01.960
and their drama.

00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:07.110
While these articles may have gotten clicks,
they took away from Vox’s credibility as

00:08:07.110 --> 00:08:10.550
the informative and reputable digital media
source.

00:08:10.550 --> 00:08:14.300
In the grand scheme of things though, this
was the smallest of their offenses.

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:20.750
The real critic of Vox is that they tell one
sided stories.

00:08:20.750 --> 00:08:26.040
ONE SIDED:
I didn’t want to make this video political,

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:31.159
but that’s impossible to achieve when Vox
themselves is so political.

00:08:31.159 --> 00:08:35.390
Basically every video they post seemingly
has an underlying liberal agenda, and this

00:08:35.390 --> 00:08:37.980
dates back to their very first videos.

00:08:37.980 --> 00:08:41.290
How Obamacare will change health insurance
forever.

00:08:41.290 --> 00:08:45.230
Competent women are getting bypassed by overconfident
men.

00:08:45.230 --> 00:08:47.769
Weed is not more dangerous than alcohol.

00:08:47.769 --> 00:08:53.910
I’m not suggesting that any of these stances
are right or wrong, but simply that they only

00:08:53.910 --> 00:08:56.000
cover one side of the story.

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:59.700
This doesn’t just stop at the headlines
or thumbnails either.

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:04.790
Basically every word, every sentence in the
video is carefully concocted to make you think

00:09:04.790 --> 00:09:06.170
a certain way.

00:09:06.170 --> 00:09:11.430
When in reality, real journalism should focus
on providing an objective view of all sides.

00:09:11.430 --> 00:09:15.870
I mean, this is one of the biggest problems
with traditional media as well.

00:09:15.870 --> 00:09:21.060
You’ve got your liberal outlets like CNN,
The New York Times, and NBC, and you’ve

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:25.709
got your conservative outlets like Fox, The
New York Post, and Newsmax.

00:09:25.709 --> 00:09:30.170
Given that one of Vox’s goals was to make
up for the shortfalls legacy media, you would

00:09:30.170 --> 00:09:34.630
think that they would’ve spent a lot of
effort in gathering on conveying diverse viewpoints.

00:09:34.630 --> 00:09:40.120
But, instead, it seems like Vox has simply
become a traditional media outlet that’s

00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:42.430
good at social media and the internet.

00:09:42.430 --> 00:09:47.709
But, despite the new medium, Vox has all of
the same issues as traditional media.

00:09:47.709 --> 00:09:51.160
And, it’s not just liberal viewpoints that
they support either.

00:09:51.160 --> 00:09:54.610
They even go as far as defending people from
the left.

00:09:54.610 --> 00:09:58.589
One of the best examples of this is with a
journalist named Sarah Jeong.

00:09:58.589 --> 00:10:01.940
In 2019, Sarah made the following remarks
on Twitter.

00:10:01.940 --> 00:10:06.790
“Oh man it’s kind of sick how much joy
I get out of being cruel to old white men”

00:10:06.790 --> 00:10:11.529
“White people marking up the internet with
their opinions like dogs pissing on fire hydrants”

00:10:11.529 --> 00:10:14.010
and “#CancelWhitePeople.”

00:10:14.010 --> 00:10:18.160
Vox published not one but three separate articles
defending Sarah.

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:24.120
They even went as far as categorizing Sarah’s
comments as “the expressive way anti-racists

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:29.800
and minorities talk about white people”
and the arguments from the other side as “a

00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:31.850
major bullying tactic of the alt-right”.

00:10:31.850 --> 00:10:36.750
I don’t think anything else needs to be
said about this situation.

00:10:36.750 --> 00:10:41.150
Over the years, these types of situations
have simply increased in popularity leading

00:10:41.150 --> 00:10:43.180
to much of the distaste surrounding Vox.

00:10:43.180 --> 00:10:48.470
“It's incredibly ironic that "Vox" is Latin
for Voice, yet they are actively involved

00:10:48.470 --> 00:10:52.430
in the censorship and deplatforming of people
and groups they dislike..”

00:10:52.430 --> 00:10:57.030
“If I see they're the source, then I always
backcheck the information.

00:10:57.030 --> 00:11:01.130
Not a source that I'd link to other people
as being reliable.

00:11:01.130 --> 00:11:05.560
Not to say everything is wrong, just more
likely to be twisted in some way.”

00:11:05.560 --> 00:11:10.290
“Their gimmick is claiming to be objective
"explainers" of the news, but in fact they

00:11:10.290 --> 00:11:12.690
give it a strong leftward spin.”

00:11:12.690 --> 00:11:16.320
And this seems to be the primary issue with
Vox.

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:22.959
THE PARADOX OF VOX:
In the end, Vox started off as an ambitious

00:11:22.959 --> 00:11:26.660
startup that was looking to achieve something
that traditional media couldn’t.

00:11:26.660 --> 00:11:31.350
This meant conveying seemingly boring topics
in a fun and intuitive manner while maintaining

00:11:31.350 --> 00:11:33.370
objectivity and clarity.

00:11:33.370 --> 00:11:39.240
But, instead it seems that Vox has fallen
into the same trap as traditional media.

00:11:39.240 --> 00:11:42.839
They ended up choosing one side and going
deeper and deeper.

00:11:42.839 --> 00:11:48.430
At this point, they’re so far to one side
that they believe that centrism is actually

00:11:48.430 --> 00:11:51.010
the media’s biggest bias.

00:11:51.010 --> 00:11:55.209
And I think this one reddit comment describes
the situation the best.

00:11:55.209 --> 00:11:56.790
“Its pseudo-news.

00:11:56.790 --> 00:12:02.160
Yes it conveys facts and explains current
events, but it does so with a bias and they

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:04.060
often cherry pick evidence.

00:12:04.060 --> 00:12:08.029
Good entertainment, but not something you
should watch if you're genuinely trying to

00:12:08.029 --> 00:12:10.200
inform yourself on a topic.”

00:12:10.200 --> 00:12:11.990
And that’s what happened to Vox.

00:12:11.990 --> 00:12:17.410
I do gotta say, despite their shortfalls,
their production quality is still insane,

00:12:17.410 --> 00:12:18.889
so respect there.

00:12:18.889 --> 00:12:21.279
Anyway, are you guys fans of Vox?

00:12:21.279 --> 00:12:22.449
Comment that down below.

00:12:22.449 --> 00:12:26.889
Also, drop a like if you would like to see
a truly objective news organization.

00:12:26.889 --> 00:12:30.839
And of course, consider checking out our discord
community to suggest future video ideas and

00:12:30.839 --> 00:12:36.480
consider subscribing to see more questions
logically answered.

